<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>


<worksheet xml:id="ws-irreducible-polynomials-activity">

	<title>Module 12: Roots, Factors, and Irreducible Polynomials</title>
	<introduction><p><alert>Unbreakable</alert></p></introduction>
	
	<page>

	<p>There are a lot of cool things about the way polynomials factor, or don't factor. This module is a collection of <q>fun facts</q> (i.e., theorems) about how polynomial factoring works.</p>
	
	<paragraphs>
		<title>The Remainder Theorem</title>
		
    <p>
  In Weekly Practice 11, we saw that, in <m>\mathbb{R}[x]</m> for
  <m>f(x) = x^{2}-3x-2</m>,
  <ul>
    <li>
      <p>
        <m>f(5) = 8</m>
      </p>
    </li>

    <li>
      <p>
        <m>f(x)= (x-5)q(x)+8</m>
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>
</p>

<exercise workspace="0.5in">
<p>
  Was it a weird coincidence that <m>f(5)=8</m> and that the remainder when
  <m>f(x)</m> was divided by <m>x-5</m> was <m>8</m>?
  Choose <m>c\in\mathbb{R}</m> and find <m>f(c)</m> and <m>r(x)</m> for which
  <m>f(x)=(x-c)q(x)+r(x)</m>.
</p>
</exercise>

<exercise workspace="0.5in">
<p>
  Let <m>F</m> be a field, <m>c\in F</m>, and <m>f(x)\in F[x]</m>.
  By the division algorithm we get for <m>f(x)</m> and <m>g(x)=x-c</m>, there
  exist unique polynomials <m>q(x)</m> and <m>r(x)</m> such that
  <md>
    f(x) = (x-c)q(x)+r(x).
  </md>
  What are the restrictions on <m>r(x)</m> given by the division algorithm?
  Why does this mean <m>r(x) = s</m> for some <m>s\in F</m>?
</p>
</exercise>

<exercise workspace="0.5in">
<p>
  What happens when you evaluate the equation in (2) at <m>x=c</m>.
  Can you conclude whether or not the example in Weekly Practice 11 and (1) were
  a coincidence?
</p>
</exercise>
    </paragraphs>
    </page>


	<page>
	
	<p>Here's a formal statement of the remainder theorem, which will help us prove the factor theorem.</p>
	
	<theorem>
  <title>The Remainder Theorem</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>F</m> be a field, let <m>p(x)\in F[x]</m>, and let <m>c\in F</m>.
      The remainder wehn <m>p(x)</m> is divided by <m>(x-c)</m> is equal to
      <m>p(c)</m>.
      That is, there exists a unique polynomial <m>q(x) \in F[x]</m> such that
      <md>
        p(x) = (x-c)q(x) +p(c).
      </md>
    </p>
  </statement>
</theorem>

	<paragraphs>
  <title>The Factor Theorem</title>

  <p>
    The factor theorem allows us to connect roots and factors.
    Here's a couple definitions.
    Let <m>R</m> be a commutative ring and let <m>p(x)\in R[x]</m>.
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          An element <m>c\in R</m> is called a
          <alert>root of the polynomial <m>p(x)</m> </alert> provided that
          <m>p(c) = 0_{R}</m>.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          A polynomial <m>f(x)</m> is a
          <alert>factor of the polynomial <m>p(x)</m> </alert> if
          <m>f(x) \mid p(x)</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>

  <theorem>
    <title>The Factor Theorem</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>F</m> be a field, let <m>p(x) \in F[x]</m> and let <m>c\in F</m>.
        Then <m>c</m> is a root of the polynomial <m>p(x)</m> if and only if
        <m>(x-c)</m> is a factor of <m>p(x)</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <p>
    Fill in the blanks of the following proof of the factor theorem.
  </p>

  <proof>
    <p>
      For the forwards direction we'll assume that
    </p>
    <!-- $c$ is a root of the polynomial -->
    <p>
      and we will show
    </p>
    <!--(x-c) is a factor of $p(x)$. -->
    <p>
      Since <m>c</m> is a root of the polynomial <m>p(x)</m>, we know <fillin fill="p(c) = 0."/>.
      <!-- $p(c) = 0$. --> By the remainder theorem, we know that there exists a
      unique polynomial <m>q(x)</m> such that
    </p>
    <p> </p>
    <!-- $p(x) = (x-c)q(x) + p(c) = (x-c)q(x) + 0$. -->
    <p>
      Thus, <m>(x-c)\mid p(x)</m> and so <m>(x-c)</m> is a factor of
      <m>p(x)</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      For the backwards direction, we will assume that
      <!--$(x-c)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ -->
    </p>

    <p>
      and show that <!--$c$ is a root of $p(x)$. -->
    </p>

    <p>
      Since <m>(x-c)</m> is a factor of <m>p(x)</m> we know <fillin fill=" (x-c) \mid p(x) "/>
      <!--$(x&#x2D;c)\mid p(x)$ --> and so there exists <m>q(x)</m> such that
      <fillin fill=" p(x) = (x-c)q(x). "/>.
      <!--$p(x) = (x&#x2D;c)q(x)$. --> Then
      <md>
        p(c) = \phantom{(c-c)q(x)=0q(x) = 0. }
      </md>
      <!--(c-c)q(x)=0q(x) = 0$. --> Thus, <m>c</m> is a root of
      <m>p(x)</m>.
    </p>
  </proof>
    </paragraphs>

	</page>
	
	<page>
	<paragraphs>
		<title>Number of Roots</title>
		
		<p>
  In this activity we'll consider how many roots a polynomial can have.
</p>

<exercise workspace="0.5in">
<p>
  What are the possibilities for number of roots of a linear function
  <m>f(x) = ax+b</m> where <m>a\neq 0</m>?
  Give an example of each possibility.
</p>
</exercise>

<exercise workspace="0.5in">
<p>
  What are the possibilities for number of roots of a quadratic function
  <m>f(x) = ax^{2}+bx+c</m> where <m>a\neq 0</m>?
  Give an example of each possibility.
</p>
</exercise>

<exercise workspace="0.5in">
<p>
  What are the possibilities for number of roots of a cubic function
  <m>f(x) = ax^{3}+bx^{2}+cx+d</m> where <m>a\neq 0</m>?
  Give an example of each possibility.
</p>
</exercise>

<exercise workspace="0.5in">
<p>
  Given a polynomial <m>f(x)</m> of degree <m>n</m>, what's the maximum number
  of roots <m>f(x)</m> can have?
</p>
</exercise>

<exercise workspace="0.5in" xml:id="num-roots-ex-5">
<p>
  Let <m>p(x) = [3]x^{2} + [3]</m> be a polynomial in <m>\mathbb{Z}_{6}[x]</m>.
  Find the roots of <m>p(x)</m> by finding <m>p(c)</m> for each
  <m>c\in \mathbb{Z}_{6}</m>.
</p>
</exercise>

<exercise workspace="0.5in">
<p>
  Why is the result of <xref ref="num-roots-ex-5" />
  surprising?
  If time: Can you factor <m>[3]x^{2} + [3]</m> over <m>\mathbb{Z}_{6}[x]</m> in
  more than one way?
</p>
</exercise>

<theorem>
  <statement>
    <p>
      A nonzero polynomial of degree <m>n\in\mathbb{N}</m> over a field has at
      most <m>n</m> roots in the field.
    </p>
  </statement>
</theorem>

<p>
  There's a proof of this in Investigation 10, page 147, Theorem 10.9, in your book.
  The proof is by induction.
</p>
	</paragraphs>
	</page>
	
	<page>
	<paragraphs>
	<title>The Fundamental Theorem of Algebra (Factoring in <m>\mathbb{C}[x]</m>)</title>
	
	<theorem>
	<title>The Fundamental Theorem of Algebra</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Every polynomial of
      degree <m>1</m> or greater in <m>\mathbb{C}[x]</m> has a root in
      <m>\mathbb{C}</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
</theorem>

<p>
  This means, every polynomial of degree <m>n\ge 1</m> in <m>\mathbb{C}[x]</m>
  has exactly <m>n</m> roots in <m>\mathbb{C}</m> (with repeated roots counted
  according to their multiplicities), and can be factored completely into linear
  factors.
  This factorization is unique up to <em>associates</em>, as you saw in the
  module prep.
</p>

	</paragraphs>
	
	<paragraphs>
	<title>Factoring in <m>\mathbb{Q}[x]</m></title>
	
	<p>
  Consider the polynomial <m>f(x) = 2x^{3} +7x^{2} +2x-3</m> in
  <m>\mathbb{Q}[x]</m>.
  Assume <m>\dfrac{p}{q}\in \mathbb{Q}</m> is a root of <m>f(x)</m> with
  <m>\dfrac{p}{q}</m> in reduced form (that is <m>\gcd(p,q)=1</m> ).
</p>

<exercise workspace="0.25in">
<p>
  Substitute <m>\dfrac{p}{q}</m> for <m>x</m> in <m>f(x)=0</m> and show that the
  equation
  <md>
    2p^{3} + 7p^{2}q+2pq^{2}-3q^{3} =0
  </md>
  results.
</p>
</exercise>

<exercise workspace="0.25in">
<p>
  Add <m>3q^{3}</m> to both sides of the equation from 1 and factor all common
  factors from <m>2p^{3}+7p^{2}q+2pq^{2}</m>.
  What does this tell us about <m>p</m>?
  (Hint: <m>\gcd(p,q)=1</m>, so what are the possible values for <m>p</m>?)
</p>
</exercise>

<exercise workspace="0.25in">
<p>
  Now subtract <m>2p^{3}</m> from both sides of the equation from 1 and factor
  all common factors from <m>7p^{2}q+2pq^{2}-3q^{3}</m>.
  What does this tell us about <m>q</m>?
</p>
</exercise>

<exercise workspace="0.25in">
<p>
  What are possible roots of <m>f(x) \in \mathbb{Q}</m>?
</p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<p>
  Find all rational roots of <m>f(x)</m>.
</p>
</exercise>
	
	</paragraphs>
	</page>
	
	
	<page>
	<p>Generalizing the previous activity gives us the rational root theorem:</p>
	
	<theorem>
  <title>The Rational Root Theorem</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      Let
      <md>
        f(x) = a_{n}x^{n} + a_{n-1}x^{n-1}+ \cdots +a_{1}x + a_{0}\in \mathbb{Z}[x]
      </md>
      with <m>a_{n}\neq 0</m>.
      If <m>r = \frac{p}{q}\in \mathbb{Q}</m> is a root of <m>f(x)</m> with
      <m>\gcd(p,q)=1</m> then <m>p\mid a_{0}</m> and <m>q\mid a_{n}</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
</theorem>

<p>
  There is one other well-known tool that is often used to determine
  irreducibility of polynomials in <m>\mathbb{Q}[x]</m>
</p>

<theorem>
	<title>Eisenstein's Criterion</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let
      <m>f(x) = a_{n}x^{n} +a_{n-1}x^{n-1}+ \cdots + a_{1}x + a_{0}\in \mathbb{Z}[x]</m>
      have degree <m>n&gt;1</m>.
      If there is a prime <m>p</m> so that <m>p\mid a_{i}</m> for
      <m>1\le i \le n-1</m>, but <m>p\nmid a_{n}</m>, and
      <m>p^{2}\nmid a_{0}</m>, then <m>f(x)</m> is irreducible in
      <m>\mathbb{Q}[x]</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
</theorem>

<p>
  Use Eisenstein's Criterion to answer the following.
</p>

<exercise workspace="1in">
<p>
  Show that <m>2x^{5} + 27x^{2} + 9x+3</m> is irreducible in
  <m>\mathbb{Q}[x]</m>.
</p>
</exercise>

<exercise workspace="1in">
<p>
  Show that for any prime <m>p</m> and any positive integer <m>n</m>, the
  polynomial <m>f(x)=x^{n} + p</m> is irreducible in <m>\mathbb{Q}[x]</m>.
  Conclude that there are irreducible polynomials in <m>\mathbb{Q}[x]</m> of any
  degree.</p>
  <p>(This is interesting because the only irreducible polynomials in <m>\mathbb{R}[x]</m> have degree 1 or 2, see weekly practice #1 in this module.)</p>
</exercise>
	</page>


<page xml:id="sec-irreducible-polynomials-review">
<paragraphs>
<title>Review questions</title>
Make sure you can answer the following questions:

<ul>
	<li><p>What are some important theorems from this module and what do they say? (The Remainder Theorem, The Factor Theorem, The Number of Roots Theorem, The Fundamental Theorem of Algebra...)</p></li>
	<li><p>Which of the theorems only apply to fields? I.e., what are some ways high school algebra get <q>broken</q> when you aren't thinking about fields?</p></li>
	<li><p>What are some tricks for factoring over <m>\mathbb{Q}[x]</m>? How might we factor over small finite fields like <m>\mathbb{Z}_p[x]</m> for primes <m>p</m>?</p></li>
</ul>
                
<p>The rest of this page is for you to summarize important ideas from the module.</p>

</paragraphs>

</page>


<page>

<paragraphs>
    <title>Weekly Practice 12</title>
	
		<exercise number="1">
		<title>Roots in <m>\mathbb{C}[x]</m> and <m>\mathbb{R}[x]</m></title>
		<introduction><p>
  The Fundamental Theorem of Algebra says any polynomial of degree
  <m>n \ge 1</m> has a root in <m>\mathbb{C}</m>.
  When you use the quadratic formula to find the roots of <m>f(x)</m>, you
  sometimes get two roots that are in <m>\mathbb{C}\backslash\mathbb{R}</m>,
  which means that <m>f(x)</m> does not have a root in <m>\mathbb{R}</m>.
  Suppose <m>z = a+bi\in \mathbb{C}</m> is a root of <m>f(x)</m>.
  Then it turns out <m>\overline{z}=a-bi</m>, the complex conjugate of <m>z</m>
  is also a root of <m>f(x)</m>.
  (There's a proof of this in Investigation 11, Theorem 11.6, on p. 157 in your book.)
</p></introduction>
        	<task><p>
  Suppose <m>z = a+bi\in\mathbb{C}</m> is a root of <m>f(x)</m>.
  Then <m>\overline{z}= a-bi</m> is also a root of <m>f(x)</m> (assume this
  without proof).
  Show that
  <md>
    (x-z)(x-\overline{z})
  </md>
  is a polynomial of degree <m>2</m> in <m>\mathbb{R}[x]</m>.
</p></task>
            <task><p>
  Conclude that if <m>f(x) \in \mathbb{R}[x]</m> is an irreducible polynomial,
  then <m>f(x)</m> has degree <m>1</m> or <m>2</m>.
  (Use the fundamental theorem of algebra and part (a).)
</p></task>
		</exercise>
		
		<exercise number="2">
		<title>Rational roots</title>
		<introduction><p>Use the rational roots theorem to do the following:</p>
		</introduction>
		
		<task><p>
  Let <m>f(x) = 18x^{3} +9x^{2} -5x-2\in \mathbb{Q}[x]</m>.
  List all possible rational roots of <m>f(x)</m>.
  Then factor <m>f(x) = 18x^{3} +9x^{2} -5x-2</m> into irreducibles.
  in <m>\mathbb{Q}[x]</m>.
</p></task>
		
		<task><p>
  Is <m>f(x) = 6x^{3} -7x^{2} +x-1</m> irreducible in <m>\mathbb{Q}[x]</m>?
  Explain.
</p></task>
		</exercise>
	
		<exercise number="3">
		<title>Factoring in <m>\mathbb{Z}_5</m></title>
		<p>
  To factor polynomials over fields other than <m>\mathbb{Q}, \mathbb{R}</m> or
  <m>\mathbb{C}</m>, trial and error is often the best option.
  We can first look for roots, which (by the Factor Theorem) give us linear
  factors.
  If we find roots, we can then divide by the corresponding linear factor to
  reduce the degree and look for roots again.</p>
  <p>Use this process to factor
  <md>
    f(x) = [3]x^{3} + [4]x^{2} + [3]
  </md>
  in <m>\mathbb{Z}_{5}[x]</m>.
</p>
		</exercise>
	</paragraphs>
	
 </page>
 
